-- Passage rg11-authors-purpose-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cb722d6f-3920-5e60-acc2-b1afa62fdcee', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$In Praise of Maintenance$q$, $q$From remarks delivered by retired bridge inspector Marisol Quintero at the reopening of a renovated footbridge in the town of Alder Falls.

Nobody throws a party for a bridge that did not fall down. I have spent thirty-one years inspecting bridges, and I can tell you that the best day of my career looked exactly like every other day: I climbed under a span, tapped the steel with a hammer, wrote down what I heard, and went home. Nothing happened. That was the point.

We are here today because this footbridge has been repainted, its bolts replaced, and its deck resurfaced. There is no new design to admire. If you walked across it last year, you will walk across it next week and notice almost nothing different. I want to suggest that this is the most impressive thing a town can do.

Building something new is exciting. It comes with ribbons, photographs, and speeches like this one. Keeping something working is quiet. It comes with budget meetings, ladders, and a crew that starts before sunrise. Yet a bridge built with great fanfare and then ignored will rust from the inside, one wet winter at a time, until the day someone notices a crack that has been growing for a decade.

I have seen what neglect costs. I have closed bridges that could have been saved for the price of a few gallons of paint, and I have watched towns spend ten times that amount replacing them. Maintenance is not the opposite of progress. It is the reason progress lasts.

So I would like to thank people whose names will never appear on a plaque: the painters who worked through a cold April, the welder who checked every joint twice, and the council members who voted for an expense that no voter would ever see. You have given this town something invisible and priceless. You have given it a bridge that will still be here, quietly doing its job, long after all of us have forgotten this afternoon.$q$, 'draft')
