   $q$How do the opening sentence and the final sentence work together to make the speech persuasive?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both predict that the footbridge will eventually fail, so the speech warns listeners not to trust the repairs that were just made.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Both use humor to lighten a serious subject, so the speech mainly aims to entertain listeners rather than to change their thinking.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"Both focus on how quickly the audience will forget the event, so the speech suggests that the reopening ceremony was a waste of time.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"Both treat an absence of drama as the real achievement, so the speech turns an event that seems unremarkable into one worth celebrating.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both treat an absence of drama as the real achievement, so the speech turns an event that seems unremarkable into one worth celebrating.$q$, $q$The opening notes that no one celebrates a bridge that "did not fall down," and the ending praises a bridge "quietly doing its job," so the frame makes invisible success the thing to admire. The opening is lightly humorous, but the speech clearly aims to change how listeners value maintenance, not just to entertain.$q$, 'draft', 'cb722d6f-3920-5e60-acc2-b1afa62fdcee')
