-- Passage rg11-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2be6111b-32d2-5cbc-9ffb-f2ed8c24afa7', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Write for the Stranger$q$, $q$From a keynote address by software developer Hiroko Tanabe at a regional student coding conference.

Somewhere, a stranger is about to read your code. Maybe it is a student two years from now, trying to fix a bug at midnight. Maybe it is a volunteer in another country who found your project and wants to help. Maybe, and this is the stranger who surprises people most, it is you, six months from today, staring at a function you wrote and no longer understand.

I want to talk about that stranger, because most of us write code as if that person does not exist.

When we are learning to program, we are graded on whether the code works. That makes sense. A program that crashes is not much use to anyone. But in the working world, code that runs is the beginning of the job, not the end. Software is rarely written once and left alone. It is read, repaired, extended, and borrowed for years, often by people its author never meets.

So the most important skill you can build is not cleverness. It is clarity. Choose variable names that say what they hold. Write a short comment explaining why a tricky line exists, not just what it does. Keep a plain file that tells a newcomer how to install and run your project. None of this is glamorous. None of it will win a contest. All of it will decide whether your work survives.

Early in my career, I wrote a program I was proud of. It was fast and compact, and only I understood it. When I left that job, the team that inherited it spent three weeks trying to decipher it before giving up and starting over. Three weeks of other people's lives, gone, because I thought explanations were beneath me.

Write for the stranger. Someday, that stranger will be grateful to you, even if you never meet.$q$, 'draft')
