-- Passage rg11-authors-purpose-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e333b01d-90fc-50c6-ae92-812d90d513e0', 'rg11-authors-purpose-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g11-authors-purpose'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Before the Voices Go Quiet$q$, $q$An essay by archivist Priya Raman, written for members of the Marlow County Historical Society.

In the basement of the Marlow County Historical Society, there are eleven boxes labeled "Telephone Exchange, 1920 to 1965." I have opened every one. They contain shift schedules, equipment invoices, a few faded photographs, and a manual explaining how to connect calls on a switchboard. What they do not contain is a single sentence written by an operator.

That absence is not unusual. For much of history, the records that survived were the ones kept by people in charge. Payrolls survived. Board meeting minutes survived. The memories of the young women who sat at the switchboards for nine hours a day, connecting a farmer to a doctor during a storm or a worried mother to a long-distance call, were rarely written down by anyone.

Some of those women are still living. I have met four of them. One, now ninety-three, told me how operators learned to recognize regular callers by voice and would quietly tell a family when the doctor was already out on another call. Another described the night the river flooded and the exchange stayed open until dawn because the operators refused to leave. None of this appears in the boxes.

The society's staff have proposed a modest oral history project: recorded interviews, transcribed and stored where students and researchers can find them. The cost is roughly what the society spends each year on new display cases. Some board members would prefer to wait until a larger grant comes through.

I understand the wish to do things properly. But a display case can be bought next year or the year after. A ninety-three-year-old's memory cannot be postponed. Every season we wait is a season in which some part of this story disappears for good, and no grant, however generous, can buy it back.

History is not only what was filed. It is also what was lived. We still have a few years to record the difference.$q$, 'draft')
